4, 0.005931854248046875, 0.005077362060546875, 0.01441192626953125, 0.0308990478515625, -0.015838623046875, -0.01123809814453125, 0.019989013671875, -0.0198211669921875, 0.00322723388671875, 0.0038280487060546875, 0.003936767578125, 0.00788116455078125, 0.0278167724609375, -0.0182952880859375, 0.0047760009765625, 0.028717041015625, 0.0159454345703125, 0.002979278564453125, -0.0113677978515625, -0.00229644775390625, -0.023529052734375, 0.0161895751953125, -0.034210205078125, 0.01323699951171875, -0.006298065185546875, -0.0228424072265625, -0.00281524658203125, 0.00620269775390625, -0.0162506103515625, -0.0028591156005859375, 0.04278564453125, 0.0266265869140625, 0.022491455078125, -0.02435302734375, -0.01081085205078125, 0.007778167724609375, 0.004634857177734375, -0.01528167724609375, -0.0026397705078125, -0.0199432373046875, 0.002193450927734375, -0.033721923828125, 0.0062255859375, -0.007205963134765625, -0.01464080810546875, 0.0316162109375, -0.0479736328125, -0.0028247833251953125, -0.0159149169921875, -0.02935791015625, 0.01528167724609375, -0.0203704833984375, -0.01264190673828125, -0.0008645057678222656], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that incrementally augment a textual memory bank while learning from a sequence of tasks\u2014have shown impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work often overlooks critical reliability aspects, such as robustness to randomness, the variance across runs, and dependence on task presentation order. These factors are important because they affect the reproducibility and trustworthiness of reported improvements and may hide failure cases or brittleness in deployment.\n\nIn this work, we address these overlooked aspects by re-evaluating two representative memory-based self-improving methods: (1) Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA) as introduced in [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR) from [Lee & Chen, 2023]. Experiments are conducted in the MiniGrid and BabyAI environments, focusing on multi-step navigation and object interaction tasks. Evaluation metrics include final episodic reward, success rate, and variance in agent performance across runs. We evaluate on both fixed and randomly shuffled task orders. For each experimental setting, we report the mean and standard deviation over five independent runs with different random seeds, providing concrete quantification of variability.\n\nOur results reveal two central indicators of fragility\u2014here defined as high sensitivity to experimental randomness or modest changes in task sequence. First, we observe that the improvement claimed by self-improving agents is highly variable: for instance, in BabyAI, the performance of EMA fluctuates by up to 18% (\u00b17.6% standard deviation in final success rate) over different runs, and the gap widens further (up to 26%) under task shuffling. This demonstrates the amplified noise introduced by the iterative self-improving loop, defined as the feedback cycle where agents update their memory and strategies based on their own past outputs. Second, agent performance dramatically deteriorates when task order is randomized\u2014e.g., RMR\u2019s average reward drops from 0.83 (ordered) to 0.54 (shuffled)\u2014highlighting that many methods rely on implicit curricula in default orderings not reported in prior work.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examined agent memory contents after each run by reviewing logged episode summaries and memory inserts, searching for dropped objectives or misaligned strategies. Criteria for analysis included consistency with task specifications, alignment of stored steps with success signals, and the presence of incomplete or ambiguous memory traces. This revealed frequent failure cases when task or environment details were ambiguous or incompletely specified\u2014a situation we define as \u201cunderspecification.\u201d \n\nIn response, we enhanced the memory construction process for both methods by integrating detailed evaluation rubrics (explicit step-by-step grading criteria derived from the environment) and explicit success/failure feedback for each episode. These augmentations reduced, but did not eliminate, the performance drop under random task sequencing (e.g., in BabyAI, EMA\u2019s post-augmentation performance gap decreased to 8.4%, but a significant deficit persisted). This suggests that while underspecification is a major driver of fragility, other latent factors remain.\n\nBased on our findings, we recommend the following actionable steps for researchers in this area: (1) Always report agent performance over multiple independent runs and include variance statistics; (2) Systematically test robustness by shuffling task order and reporting those results; (3) When presenting new agent designs, clearly define and mitigate possible sources of underspecification through explicit documentation or rubric-sharing; and (4) Develop standardized human-in-the-loop oversight tools that interface with agent memory, enabling rapid detection of failure modes uncovered in this study. We believe these steps are necessary for reliable deployment and meaningful evaluation of online self-improving agents."}}